    id: "90373cf6cc69697cd8c8",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "'You're not alone in the darkness': Gambia president urges calm after violent blackout protests",
    summary: "The country has faced persistent blackouts since June, with some lasting up to 48 hours, residents say.",
    preview: "The Gambia's President Adama Barrow has urged citizens to remain calm over prolonged power cuts, saying the country is not alone in facing an electricity crisis that has affected several parts of West Africa. His comments follow protests on Monday night in the capital Banjul and surrounding areas over blackouts that residents say have sometimes lasted up to 48 hours. \"When you lie down in the heat and the dark, believe that you are not alone in that darkness,\" Barrow said, pledging to boost power supplies by next month.",
    body: "The Gambia's President Adama Barrow has urged citizens to remain calm over prolonged power cuts, saying the country is not alone in facing an electricity crisis that has affected several parts of West Africa.\n\nHis comments follow protests on Monday night in the capital Banjul and surrounding areas over blackouts that residents say have sometimes lasted up to 48 hours.\n\n\"When you lie down in the heat and the dark, believe that you are not alone in that darkness,\" Barrow said, pledging to boost power supplies by next month.\n\nThe Gambia supplements its own generation with imported electricity, including supplies from Guinea and neighbouring Senegal.\n\nThe tiny West African country has endured persistent blackouts since June, with outages becoming longer and more frequent during the current hot season as soaring temperatures drive up electricity demand.\n\nFrustration boiled over on Monday night, when protesters took to the streets in Banjul and surrounding areas, burning tyres and blocking roads over the prolonged power cuts.\n\nPolice used tear gas to disperse crowds in Westfield area as they moved towards the headquarters of the national electricity company, Nawec, with some chanting. \"Barrow must go\".\n\nProtesters also gathered outside the vice-president's residence in Farato, where banners at an office of Barrow's ruling National People's Party were damaged.\n\nThe unrest comes just months before a presidential election expected in December, in which Barrow is seeking a controversial third term.\n\nIn a televised address on Tuesday night, Barrow acknowledged the hardship caused by the blackouts but warned protesters against damaging public infrastructure, saying it would only worsen the disruption and divert money towards repairs.\n\n\"Let us not destroy in a moment of anger what we suffered so long to gain,\" he said.\n\nThe president admitted there had been shortcomings in the government's planning, power generation and maintenance systems, calling the resulting outages \"a national security issue\" and an \"emergency\".\n\n\"I know that the fans have stopped turning, children work in the dark, mothers throw away stale food, and the heat is unbearable by day and by night,\" the president, promising that supplies would improve by the end of October.\n\nHe said a new 24-megawatt generating unit was being installed at the Brikama power station and would be commissioned by the end of next month.\n\nA contract has also been awarded for a 50MW solar power plant in Soma, although no completion date was given.\n\n\"For a system of our size, this is considerable power, enough to make a big difference in your homes,\" Barrow added.\n\nThe president visited facilities for the national energy utility on Tuesday morning and promised that The Gambia would receive new generators this week.\n\nGallo Saidy, the Nawec chief, also appealed for patience, saying reduced electricity imports from the regional grid had contributed to the shortages.\n\nHe said lower rainfall this year had reduced the electricity available through the regional network.\n\n\"We are aware of the frustration and constraints that the Gambian people have,\" he said. \"We are just asking The Gambian people to give us a little bit of time and patience.\"\n\nNawec had warned in August that unusually high temperatures had driven a surge in electricity demand, while a technical problem had also affected a major generating unit supplying imported power.\n\nYou may also be interested in:\n\n- West Africa signs off $25bn mega gas-pipeline plan\n\n- Published20 July\n\n- Gambia probes sale of ex-leader's luxury cars, cows and boats\n\n- Published15 May 2025\n\n- The pride of Ethiopia - What it took to build Africa's largest hydro-electric dam\n\n- Published9 September 2025\n\n- The witch hunts which left a nation traumatised\n\n- Published12 June 2022\n\nGo to BBCAfrica.com, external for more news from the African continent.\n\nFollow us on Twitter @BBCAfrica, external, on Facebook at BBC Africa, external or on Instagram at bbcafrica, external",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "BBC World",
    url: "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cgmr22kpd9yo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
